"""Build reviewed written questions, including both alternatives in each ERQ."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]

def code(value):return '```python\n'+value+'\n```'
rows={'XI':[],'XII':[]}
def add(roman,n,page,marks,slos,stem,answer):
 rows[roman].append(dict(number=n,page=page,marks=marks,slos=slos.split(),stem=stem,answer=answer))
add('XI','1',2,3,'1.6.2','A school develops a customised school management and information system for student records, attendance and fees. It deploys the system on a cloud platform that handles infrastructure and scaling, leaving developers to code and test.\n\ni. Identify the service model. (1 mark)\nii. Explain with a reason and give one real-world example. (2 marks)','Platform as a Service (PaaS). The provider manages the hosting platform, runtime and underlying infrastructure while developers deploy their own application code. Google App Engine is an example. The customised application is developed by the school, so the scenario is not simply subscribing to a ready-made application.')
add('XI','2',2,3,'1.8.2','Fatima notices students downloading untrusted files, using weak passwords and leaving devices unattended. A school computer suffers ransomware that encrypts student records. Suggest THREE effective cybersecurity measures.','1. Restrict downloads to trusted sources and keep anti-malware software and operating systems updated.\n2. Enforce strong unique passwords and automatic screen locking. Add multi-factor authentication where available.\n3. Maintain regular, tested backups with an offline or otherwise isolated copy so ransomware cannot encrypt every recovery copy.')
add('XI','3',3,3,'1.9.2','i. Explain the relationship between cryptography and encryption. (2 marks)\nii. Explain how they differ and work together to secure digital communication, with an example. (1 mark)','Cryptography is the broader field of protecting information using mathematical techniques. Encryption is one cryptographic operation that transforms plaintext into ciphertext using an algorithm and key. Other cryptographic techniques provide authentication and integrity. For example, HTTPS uses TLS with encryption to protect message confidentiality and authentication and integrity mechanisms to establish identities and detect tampering.')
add('XI','4',3,3,'2.2.1','Write an algorithm to search for 10 in [5, 10, 15, 20].','1. Set values to [5, 10, 15, 20] and target to 10.\n2. Visit each index in order.\n3. If values[index] equals target, report the index and stop.\n4. If the loop finishes without a match, report not found.\n\nThe first comparison checks 5. The second finds 10 at index 1, which is position 2.')
add('XI','5',4,3,'3.1.2','Differentiate between syntax and semantics in programming. Give one example of each.','Syntax consists of the grammatical rules for writing a program. For example, a Python if statement requires a colon and an indented body. Semantics concerns the meaning or behaviour of a statement. For example, x = x + 1 means to calculate the old value plus one and assign it to x. A statement can be syntactically valid while computing the wrong intended result.')
add('XI','6',4,3,'3.7.2','Write a Python program that takes a temperature in °C and displays Cold (≤15), Moderate (16–30), or Hot (>30), using if-elif-else.',code('temperature = int(input("Temperature in °C: "))\nif temperature <= 15:\n    print("Cold")\nelif temperature <= 30:\n    print("Moderate")\nelse:\n    print("Hot")')+'\n\nThis solution accepts integer temperatures, matching the printed ranges. The boundaries 15, 16, 30 and 31 produce Cold, Moderate, Moderate and Hot.')
add('XI','7',5,3,'4.1.4','A bank predicts the chance of loan repayment from income, age, loan history and spending.\ni. Analyse the model’s effectiveness for loan applications. (1 mark)\nii. Give ONE advantage and ONE limitation. (2 marks)','The model can estimate repayment risk consistently from relevant historical patterns, supporting lending decisions when validated on representative data.\n\nAdvantage — it can process many applications quickly and consistently.\n\nLimitation — biased or outdated training data can produce unfair or inaccurate estimates. Its output should support accountable human review and periodic validation.')
add('XI','8a',6,7,'3.9.2 3.8.3','i. Correct this code to print the numbers 1 to 5. (3 marks)\n\n```python\nfor i in range(1,5)\n    print i\n```\n\nii. Use nested for loops to print the following pattern. (4 marks)\n\n```text\n4321\n432\n43\n1\n```',code('for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)')+'\n\nrange excludes its upper bound, so 6 includes 5. The colon and print parentheses are required.\n\n'+code('for width in range(4, 0, -1):\n    for column in range(width):\n        if width == 1:\n            print(1, end="")\n        else:\n            print(4 - column, end="")\n    print()')+'\n\nThe final line reproduces the source’s printed 1. This is an irregular pattern, so the final row is handled explicitly.')
add('XI','8b',6,7,'3.6.3 3.7.2','Develop a grocery billing program that takes prices and quantities for two items, calculates each item total and the grand total, applies a 10% discount when the grand total exceeds Rs 1000, displays the calculated values, and identifies the item with the greater total cost.',code('price1 = float(input("Price of item 1: "))\nquantity1 = int(input("Quantity of item 1: "))\nprice2 = float(input("Price of item 2: "))\nquantity2 = int(input("Quantity of item 2: "))\ntotal1 = price1 * quantity1\ntotal2 = price2 * quantity2\ngrand_total = total1 + total2\ndiscount = 0\nif grand_total > 1000:\n    discount = grand_total * 0.10\nprint("Item 1 total:", total1)\nprint("Item 2 total:", total2)\nprint("Grand total:", grand_total)\nprint("Discount:", discount)\nprint("Amount payable:", grand_total - discount)\nif total1 > total2:\n    print("Item 1 has the greater total cost")\nelif total2 > total1:\n    print("Item 2 has the greater total cost")\nelse:\n    print("Both item totals are equal")'))
add('XI','9a',8,7,'5.1.3','Hospitals struggle to monitor chronic conditions after discharge.\ni. Explain how IoT can address post-discharge monitoring. (2 marks)\nii. Analyse continuous monitoring, naming devices and the data they collect. (3 marks)\niii. Give an example showing how the data supports timely decisions. (2 marks)','i. Connected home or wearable sensors send patient measurements to a monitoring system for review between visits. Configured alerts can draw attention to concerning changes.\n\nii. A connected blood-pressure monitor records blood pressure, a pulse oximeter records oxygen saturation and pulse, and a glucose sensor records glucose trends. Repeated measurements can reveal deterioration earlier than occasional visits, subject to sensor accuracy, connectivity, consent and clinical interpretation.\n\niii. For example, a sustained abnormal trend may trigger an alert to a care team, which contacts the patient, checks the reading and decides the appropriate intervention. The system supports clinicians rather than making unreviewed treatment decisions.')
add('XI','9b',8,7,'6.5.2','i. Analyse TWO positive and TWO negative environmental impacts of increased connectivity. (4 marks)\nii. Give TWO supporting examples. (2 marks)\niii. Suggest responsible cloud and smart-device use to reduce harm. (1 mark)','Positive effects include reduced travel through remote communication and reduced energy waste through sensor-based control. Negative effects include electricity demand from networks and data centres and electronic waste from device replacement.\n\nFor example, remote meetings can avoid commuting, although streaming still consumes energy. A smart thermostat can reduce heating waste, while frequent replacement of incompatible smart devices adds electronic waste.\n\nUse efficient devices, keep useful equipment for longer, consolidate unnecessary cloud workloads and use approved recycling routes.')
add('XII','1',2,3,'9.2.1','a. Define Human Computer Interaction (HCI). (1 mark)\nb. State TWO important aspects of HCI when designing a digital system. (2 marks)','HCI studies and designs how people interact with computer systems. Two important aspects are usability, so users can complete tasks effectively and efficiently, and accessibility, so people with different abilities can use the system.')
add('XII','2',2,3,'10.3.4','Write a binary-search algorithm to find 32 in [7, 11, 19, 24, 32, 45, 58].','1. Set low = 0 and high = 6.\n2. While low ≤ high, set mid = floor((low + high) / 2).\n3. If values[mid] = 32, report mid and stop.\n4. If values[mid] < 32, set low = mid + 1. Otherwise set high = mid − 1.\n5. If the loop finishes, report not found.\n\nThe comparisons are 24 at index 3, 45 at index 5 and 32 at index 4. The answer is index 4, or position 5.')
add('XII','3',2,2,'11.1.5','Describe local and global variables used in functions.','A local variable is bound within a function and normally accessible only in that function’s local scope. A global variable is bound at module level and can be read by functions unless shadowed. Assigning to that global name inside a function requires a global declaration. Mutating an object referenced by a global name is distinct from rebinding the name.')
add('XII','4',3,4,'11.1.1 11.1.3','a. Define a user-defined function. (1 mark)\nb. Explain the significance of the three components of a function signature. (3 marks)','A user-defined function is a named reusable block of code written by the programmer for a particular task.\n\nThe function name identifies the callable operation. Parameters describe inputs accepted by the function. The return value describes the result supplied to the caller. In Python, the def header declares the name and parameters, while return produces a result and an optional return annotation documents its intended type.')
add('XII','5',3,3,'11.2.3','Write a Python program that calculates and returns the average of three test scores using a user-defined function.',code('def average_scores(first, second, third):\n    return (first + second + third) / 3\n\na = float(input("First score: "))\nb = float(input("Second score: "))\nc = float(input("Third score: "))\nprint("Average:", average_scores(a, b, c))'))
add('XII','6',4,3,'12.2.4','Describe an F-test, a chi-square test and ANOVA.','An F-test uses an F-distributed ratio, commonly to compare variances or assess nested statistical models under the required assumptions.\n\nA chi-square test compares observed and expected category counts, for example to assess goodness of fit or association between categorical variables.\n\nANOVA tests whether the means of groups are consistent with a common population mean by comparing between-group and within-group variation. A significant result does not identify every differing pair without further analysis.')
add('XII','7',4,3,'14.3.3','Describe substitution, Caesar and shift ciphers.','A substitution cipher replaces plaintext symbols with other symbols according to a defined mapping.\n\nA Caesar cipher traditionally shifts every letter three places around the alphabet, for example A becomes D.\n\nA general shift cipher shifts every letter by a chosen fixed key, wrapping around the alphabet. Caesar is a particular shift cipher. These classical ciphers are useful teaching examples and are unsuitable for modern confidential communications.')
add('XII','8a',5,7,'11.5.8','Write a Python program using inheritance to input and display a student’s details. Person must have name and age. Student must inherit Person and add marks.',code('class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nclass Student(Person):\n    def __init__(self, name, age, marks):\n        super().__init__(name, age)\n        self.marks = marks\n\n    def display(self):\n        print("Name:", self.name)\n        print("Age:", self.age)\n        print("Marks:", self.marks)\n\nname = input("Name: ")\nage = int(input("Age: "))\nmarks = float(input("Marks: "))\nstudent = Student(name, age, marks)\nstudent.display()'))
add('XII','8b',5,7,'11.6.5','Write a Python program that uses a for loop to store numeric values in a one-dimensional list, finds the highest, lowest and average values, and searches for a value entered by the user.',code('count = int(input("How many values? "))\nif count <= 0:\n    print("Enter at least one value")\nelse:\n    values = []\n    for index in range(count):\n        values.append(float(input("Value: ")))\n    print("Highest:", max(values))\n    print("Lowest:", min(values))\n    print("Average:", sum(values) / len(values))\n    target = float(input("Search value: "))\n    if target in values:\n        print("Found at index:", values.index(target))\n    else:\n        print("Not found")'))
add('XII','9a',7,7,'13.1.3','Describe FOUR benefits and THREE challenges of cloud computing for managing data in Pakistan.','Benefits\n1. Remote access supports distributed staff and institutions.\n2. Scalable capacity accommodates changing workloads.\n3. Managed infrastructure can reduce upfront local hardware expenditure.\n4. Off-site backups and replication can support recovery and continuity.\n\nChallenges\n1. Unreliable connectivity or electricity at the user’s premises can interrupt access.\n2. Privacy, data location and security requirements need careful provider selection and configuration.\n3. Recurring subscription and transfer charges, including currency exposure and vendor lock-in, can make long-term cost management difficult.')
add('XII','9b',7,7,'14.2.1','Name any SEVEN protocols from the provided list: SSH, SSL, TLS, IPsec, HTTP, FTPS, HTTPS, SNMP and WPA/WPA2/WPA3.','Seven valid choices from the printed list are SSH, TLS, IPsec, FTPS, HTTPS, SNMP and WPA3.\n\nSSH supports authenticated encrypted remote access. TLS protects transport connections. IPsec protects IP traffic. FTPS adds TLS to FTP. HTTPS is HTTP over TLS. SNMP manages network devices, with SNMPv3 providing security features. WPA3 protects wireless network access.\n\nThe source also lists SSL and HTTP. SSL is obsolete and HTTP alone provides no transport encryption. SNMP security depends on its version and configuration.')
for roman,items in rows.items():
 assert len(items)==11
 assert sum(q['marks'] for q in items if q['number'][-1:]!='b')==35
syll=json.loads((ROOT/'data/computer-science/hssc_syllabus_2025.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
for roman,grade in [('XI','11'),('XII','12')]:
 valid={s['code'] for u in syll[grade]['units'] for t in u['subtopics'] for s in t['slos']}
 assert all(code in valid for q in rows[roman] for code in q['slos'])
(ROOT/'data/computer-science/hssc_model_written_review.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
print('22 written alternatives with answers and valid syllabus mappings')
